510-| A1 | `off_axis_angle` constant 0.0 on all 176,223 rows | **SILENT.** A constant column enters only through the bias; the head fits normally and no loss signal distinguishes "informative feature unused" from "uninformative feature". |
511-| A2 | No elevation feature among the 16 | **SILENT.** An absent input produces no training signal at all; it shows up only as an accuracy ceiling indistinguishable from irreducible noise without the counterfactual. |
512-| A3 | `missing_incumbent` also constant 0.0 on all 176,223 rows | **SILENT**, same mechanism as A1, and doubly so: training cannot tell whether "no anchor ever has a missing incumbent" is a fact about the world or an artifact of the row builder. |
513:| A4 | 16-column design matrix has rank 14; condition number infinite (σ₁₆ = 0 exactly) | **SILENT** for the gradient-trained heads actually used — the two null directions simply never receive gradient. Would have been **visible** only under a closed-form normal-equations solve, which is not what runs. |
514:| A5 | Slots 4 and 12 are exact indicator functions of slots 3 and 11 | **SILENT.** Duplicated information never hurts fit and never appears in loss. It makes the effective input dimension 12, not 16, and makes any per-slot ablation or attribution over those pairs arbitrary. |
515-| A6 | `nominal_required_power_over_cap` = 1.000e+06 on 10.21% of rows (every null-action row); slot SD 302,837 against O(1) elsewhere | **PARTIALLY VISIBLE — the only one with a real chance.** At an ordinary learning rate this can spike or destabilise early loss. It can equally be absorbed by the first layer learning a near-zero weight on that slot, which silently deletes the legitimate power information in the other 89.79% of rows. Visible as instability if it bites; silent as information loss if it does not. Either way the loss would not name the cause. |
516:| B1 | C1 target is `κ·tanh(log gain-ratio)`, not `c1_difference_surplus`; \|c1/κ\| ≤ 1 on 100.000% of rows, 30.76% at ≥ 0.99, 10.21% exactly −1; against the declared target on the same rows, **r = 0.486 and r = 0.035** at the two diffed anchors | **SILENT, and actively misleading.** A bounded smooth monotone function of one scalar is *easier* to learn than the true unbounded bits−ηJ surplus (which reaches +8.30 κ and +11.40 κ where the surrogate caps at +1). Loss falls faster and further than it would on the declared target, so training health argues in the wrong direction. |
517:| B2 | C2 target is `κ·Σtanh(log future-ratio)` with −1 per lost offset; `c2_persistence_forecast` is never called; c2/κ ∈ [−3,+3] with 31.06% exactly at −3; against the declared target, **sign agreement only 37.7%** | **SILENT**, same mechanism as B1, with two extra pushes in the same wrong direction: ~31% of the target is a single constant, which lowers loss further; and because the surrogate disagrees with the declared persistence surplus about *which action is better* on ~3 rows in 5, a C2 head that fits this target well is not approximately right about the declared one — it is wrong more often than a coin. |
518-| B3 | `outage` agrees with `null_action` on all 176,223 rows (0 disagreements either way), so it encodes only "this is the null action"; at the diffed anchors the normal path flags **35 and 27 real PHY service failures the fallback calls non-outage**, and 0 the other way | **SILENT.** It is not a learner input; it reaches C3 only as `CoalitionMember.missing_incumbent`, which is `true` on 0 of the 360 shipped member entries. Nothing in training touches it. |
519:| B4 | Φ computed without `cell_rekeyed_users` on the fallback path | **SILENT now and SILENT later.** Measured: `c1_phi_difference` is bit-identical on every row at both diffed anchors, because `_rekeyed_users` returns `()` whenever a step carries dense arrays and all 30 anchors on this tape do. The defect is real in the code and **inert on this corpus**; it activates on tapes built from boundary candidates instead. Training would never surface either state. |
520:| B5 | Selected coalition chosen by lexicographic `configuration_id` rather than by the `−(C1+C3)` factor score, over an unfiltered rather than validity-filtered candidate set; at the two diffed anchors the rules picked **different coalitions** ({6,24} vs {6,40}; {8,17} vs **disjoint** {53,96}) with ψ = −3.0358 vs +0.0008 and +36.0147 vs +0.0000 | **SILENT.** The C3 head trains on whatever coalition it is handed; a differently-chosen coalition is a different, equally learnable supervised point, and its label differs by a sign at one anchor and four orders of magnitude at the other. The corpus-level consequence (one size-2 coalition per anchor, 91 distinct user sets over 180 rows) is a coverage fact, not a loss fact. |
