+# Exact source build completion criterion
+
+The task is complete when a four-anchor, both-path calibration reconciles the
+published timing discrepancy; the Part 1 reconciliation is printed before any
+generation begins; and the new exact-path artefact contains a clean prefix of
+the predeclared 180-anchor order, per-anchor checkpoints, an independently
+verified manifest, and the required C1 gauge, decomposition, correlation,
+rank-correlation, and argmax-disagreement measurements, without changing the
+pilot runner bytes or any sealed input.

codex
Part 1 is complete and was printed to stdout before generation.

Verified by running code: for four distinct anchors—world 1 step 0 on all three carriers, plus world 2 step 0 on `nearest-eligible`—with `include_coalition=False`, shortlist actions, one boundary `(0,)`, fresh evaluators, tape-build time excluded, and four concurrent `nice -n 15` workers, the reconciled exact rate is **112.208 s/anchor** versus **1.215 s/anchor** primitive (**92.38×**). The exact physical counts were 3,492–3,576 boundary evaluations; primitive was always 48.

Derived on paper from that measured four-process rate: **90 anchors = 2,524.7 s (42.08 min)** and **180 anchors = 5,049.3 s (84.16 min)**, before amortized tape setup.

Reconciliation: the published **49.03 s** configuration is no-coalition, one carrier per anchor, four-step tape, row-build-only, effectively single-process. The published **632.83 s** configuration used `include_coalition=True` on a full 33-step tape and counted 4,580 evaluations; its paired primitive run’s 938 evaluations explain the published 4.79 s versus 0.848 s. In this rerun, the same coalition-enabled anchor took **80.784 s exact / 6.496 s primitive**, so coalition inclusion reproduces the evaluation-count/configuration difference but not the historical 632.83-second wall time. A warm repeat of the no-coalition exact anchor took **65.807 s**; the four-way baseline ranged 101.857–120.815 s. Thus the remaining historical gap is inferred to be machine contention/cache state, not carrier count or boundaries. All tested paths used boundary `(0,)`; all three carriers showed the same workload class.

The declared generation order is now fixed: **world 1, steps 0–29, carrier order `nearest-eligible`, `stay-if-possible`, then the third engine-declared reference carrier; then world 2 in the same step/carrier order**—180 anchors total. At the reconciled rate, all 180 fit the five-hour budget.
